: The input reading loop uses fs.nextInt() inside nested loops; that's okay but we might want to read row into a temporary buffer then compute prefix, as C did with vector<int> row. That could improve cache locality? Actually computing directly from scanner is fine.\n\nLet's write final code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdio>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <climits>\n\nclass FastScanner {\n    static constexpr int BUFSIZE = 1 << 20;\n    char buf[BUFSIZE];\n    char* cur;\n    char* end;\n\n    bool refill() {\n        size_t r = fread(buf, 1, BUFSIZE, stdin);\n        cur = buf;\n        end = buf + r;\n        return r > 0;\n    }\n\npublic:\n    FastScanner() : cur(buf), end(buf) {}\n\n    inline int nextInt() {\n        while (true) {\n            if (cur == end && !refill()) return 0;\n            char c = *cur++;\n            if (c <= ' ') continue;\n\n            int sign = 1;\n            if (c == '-') {\n                sign = -1;\n                if (cur == end && !refill()) return 0;\n                c = *cur++;\n            }\n\n            int x = c - '0';\n            while (true) {\n                if (cur == end && !refill()) break;\n                char d = *cur++;\n                if (d < '0' || d > '9') break;\n                x = x * 10 + (d - '0');\n            }\n            return sign * x;\n        }\n    }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    FastScanner fs;\n    int n = fs.nextInt();\n    int s = fs.nextInt();\n\n    const size_t N = static_cast<size_t>(n);\n    std::vector<long long> pref((N + 1) * N, 0);\n\n    for (size_t i = 1; i <= N; ++i) {\n        const size_t base = i * N;\n        const size_t prev = (i - 1) * N;\n        for (size_t j = 0; j < N; ++j) {\n            int v = fs.nextInt();\n            pref[base + j] = pref[prev + j] + v;\n        }\n    }\n\n    long long mxSum = LLONG_MIN;\n    const size_t limit_i = N - static_cast<size_t>(s);\n\n    #pragma omp parallel for reduction(max:mxSum) schedule(static)\n    for (size_t i = 0; i <= limit_i; ++i) {\n        const long long* top = &pref[i * N];\n        const long long* bottom = &pref[(i + s) * N];\n\n        long long window = 0;\n        #pragma omp simd reduction(+:window)\n        for (size_t j = 0; j < static_cast<size_t>(s); ++j) {\n            window += bottom[j] - top[j];\n        }\n        long long curMax = window;\n\n        // Sliding horizontal window\n        for (size_t j = static_cast<size_t>(s); j < N; ++j) {\n            window += (bottom[j] - top[j]) - (bottom[j - s] - top[j - s]);\n            if (window > curMax) curMax = window;\n        }\n\n        mxSum = std::max(mxSum, curMax);\n    }\n\n    printf(\"%lld\\n\", mxSum);\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\nThis integrates:\n- A's FastScanner.\n- B's OpenMP parallel for reduction(max:mxSum).\n- C's vertical prefix sums and SIMD on initial window accumulation.\n\nWe should also consider unrolling the sliding loop as in A? Could add manual unroll by 4 to improve. Let's do that:\n\n```cpp\n        size_t j = static_cast<size_t>(s);\n        for (; j + 3 < N; j += 4) {\n            window += (bottom[j] - top[j]) - (bottom[j - s] - top[j - s]);\n            if (window > curMax) curMax = window;\n            window += (bottom[j+1] - top[j+1]) - (bottom[j+1-s] - top[j+1-s]);\n            if (window > curMax) curMax = window;\n            window += (bottom[j+2] - top[j+2]) - (bottom[j+2-s] - top[j+2-s]);\n            if (window > curMax) curMax = window;\n            window += (bottom[j+3] - top[j+3]) - (bottom[j+3-s] - top[j+3-s]);\n            if (window > curMax) curMax = window;\n        }\n        for (; j < N; ++j) {\n            window += (bottom[j] - top[j]) - (bottom[j - s] - top[j - s]);\n            if (window > curMax) curMax = window;\n        }\n```\n\nBut careful: `j+1-s` etc. indices valid since j>=s and j<N, so j+3 < N ensures j+3-s >=0? Since j>=s, yes.\n\nThis adds unrolling from A's transformation style.\n\nAlso we can add `#pragma omp simd` on the sliding loop? Not possible due to dependency.\n\nLet's include that.\n\nNow check correctness: For each i, window starts as sum of first s columns' vertical differences. Then slides right by adding new column and subtracting leftmost. This computes all SxS submatrix sums for row start i. Works.\n\nEdge cases: n=1? But s<n so n>=2. limit_i = n-s >=1. Good.\n\nMemory: pref size (n+1)*n long longs; for n=8000 ~512MB. Might be okay but we can reduce by using int if we assume values small? To be safe, keep long long.\n\nWe should also consider that the input reading loop may benefit from `#pragma omp simd` on j? But fs.nextInt() is sequential and cannot vectorize; removing it.\n\nNow produce JSON with files array containing src/solution.cpp content. sources_kept: we must include only experiment IDs from integration_source_ids, which are all three: [\"aec6b1a40d3046d2\", \"cf2feb9778ae44fc\", \"cb6ca801faf74dd3\"]. We can keep all three because we used ideas from each.\n\nTransformations list should describe what we did:\n- Integrated A's buffered scanner with direct buffer cursors.\n- Used C's vertical prefix sums and SIMD reduction for initial window accumulation.\n- Added B's OpenMP parallelization over starting rows with reduction(max:mxSum).\n- Unrolled the horizontal sliding-window loop by four.\n\nHypothesis: Combining fast I/O from A, column-prefix-sum sliding windows from C, and row-parallel OpenMP reduction from B yields lower runtime than any single archived transformation by reducing input parsing overhead while exploiting multi-core parallelism and vectorization.\n\nNotes: mention correctness considerations (long long to avoid overflow), memory usage.\n\nLet's write final code with unrolling. Also ensure we include <cstdio> for printf/fread, <vector>, <algorithm>, <climits>.\n\nOne more thing: The original baseline used `std::max`; we use std::max from algorithm.\n\nNow produce JSON."
